---
// Reference / Error codes.
//
// The registry itself already ships at /e/, one page per code, grouped by class
// on its index. That is the shape Postgres uses for its error appendix and it
// works. What was missing is the half Stripe spends four thousand words on:
// what to actually do when you get one.
//
// So this page is the handling contract rather than a second copy of the list.
// It leans on the two fields that make Strata's registry more useful than the
// ones I compared it against: neither Stripe nor Postgres tells you whether the
// operation took effect, and this one does, per code.
//
// Every count is computed from the registry rather than written down, which is
// what keeps the page honest when codes are added, removed or reclassified.
// 1.2.0 reclassified two of them and broke a build over it.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import registry from '../../../data/error-registry.json';
import index from '../../../data/command-index.json';

interface ErrorEntry {
  class: string;
  code: string;
  commit_outcome: string;
  hint: string;
  message: string;
  retry_policy: string;
}
const errors = registry.errors as ErrorEntry[];

const tally = (key: 'retry_policy' | 'commit_outcome') => {
  const counts = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const e of errors) counts.set(e[key], (counts.get(e[key]) ?? 0) + 1);
  return [...counts.entries()].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
};

const classes = new Set(errors.map((e) => e.class)).size;
const ambiguous = errors.filter((e) => e.commit_outcome === 'maybe_committed');

// Which codes a catalogued command can raise, so the page can say how much of
// the registry is reachable from the command surface.
const raised = new Set<string>();
for (const command of index.commands) {
  for (const e of command.errors ?? []) raised.add(e.code);
}

const RETRY_MEANING: Record<string, string> = {
  never:
    'Retrying the same request will fail the same way. Something has to change first, usually your input.',
  same_request: 'The identical request is worth retrying, typically after a backoff.',
  after_state_change:
    'Retry once the condition has changed: reload the current state, or wait for the resource to exist.',
  unknown: 'Strata cannot say. Treat it as unsafe to retry blindly and reconcile first.',
};
const OUTCOME_MEANING: Record<string, string> = {
  not_started: 'The command was rejected before it touched anything. Nothing was written.',
  not_applicable: 'The command does not write, so there is nothing to have committed.',
  definitely_not_committed: 'The command started and did not commit. Nothing was written.',
  maybe_committed: 'Strata does not know whether the write landed. Check before acting.',
};

const toc = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'shape', text: 'What a code looks like' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'act', text: 'The two fields worth branching on' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'ambiguous', text: 'When Strata does not know' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'find', text: 'Finding a code' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Error codes"
  description="Every failure carries a typed code that says whether a retry is safe and whether the write landed."
  section="Reference"
  wide
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Error codes</h1>
  <p>
    Every failure in Strata carries a code. Not a message you match on, and not a number you look up
    in a table somewhere: a stable identifier that says what kind of failure it was, whether
    retrying is safe, and whether your write took effect.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last part is the unusual one. Most error registries tell you what went wrong. This one also
    tells you what happened to your data, which is the question you actually need answered before
    you decide what to do next.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shape">What a code looks like</h2>
  <p>Codes read left to right, from the kind of problem to the specific case:</p>
  <pre
    class="not-prose"><code>conflict.engine.promotion
│        │      └ the specific failure
│        └ the component that raised it
└ the class, one of {classes}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Match on the code, or on its class prefix if you want to handle a whole family at once. Do not
    match on the message: messages are written for people and are free to change, while codes are
    the contract.
  </p>

  <h2 id="act">The two fields worth branching on</h2>
  <p>Every entry in the registry carries both, and between them they decide what you do next.</p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 text-body-lg font-medium text-ink-hi">retry_policy</h3>
  <dl class="not-prose mt-3 space-y-4">
    {
      tally('retry_policy').map(([value, count]) => (
        <div class="border-l border-line pl-4">
          <dt class="font-mono text-mono-body text-ink-hi">
            {value} <span class="font-sans text-small text-ink-low">· {count} codes</span>
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-[46rem] text-body text-ink-mid">{RETRY_MEANING[value]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h3 class="mt-8 text-body-lg font-medium text-ink-hi">commit_outcome</h3>
  <dl class="not-prose mt-3 space-y-4">
    {
      tally('commit_outcome').map(([value, count]) => (
        <div class="border-l border-line pl-4">
          <dt class="font-mono text-mono-body text-ink-hi">
            {value} <span class="font-sans text-small text-ink-low">· {count} codes</span>
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-[46rem] text-body text-ink-mid">{OUTCOME_MEANING[value]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <p class="mt-8">
    A caller that reads these two fields can recover automatically without guessing. A conflict on a
    merge says <code>never</code> and <code>not_started</code>: your target branch is untouched, and
    repeating the identical request will fail identically, so reload and decide. A closed runtime
    says <code>after_state_change</code>: the same call will work once the runtime is open again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ambiguous">When Strata does not know</h2>
  <p>
    {ambiguous.length} of the {errors.length} codes report{' '}
    <code>maybe_committed</code>. They are worth knowing by name, because they are the only ones
    where reading the error is not enough:
  </p>
  <ul class="not-prose mt-4 space-y-3">
    {
      ambiguous.map((e) => (
        <li class="rounded-(--radius-frame) border border-line bg-raised px-5 py-4">
          <a
            href={`/e/${e.code}`}
            class="font-mono text-mono-body text-terracotta-500 transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-terracotta-400"
          >
            {e.code}
          </a>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body text-ink-mid">{e.hint}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-6">
    Both are transport and persistence failures where the write may have landed before the answer
    was lost. There is an error class named <code>ambiguous_commit</code> for exactly this, which is a
    deliberate choice: a system that cannot tell you should say so rather than guess. Read the record
    back before retrying, or make the write idempotent so that retrying is harmless either way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="find">Finding a code</h2>
  <p>
    The full registry is at <a href="/e/">the error index</a>, grouped by class, with a page per
    code carrying its message, hint, retry policy and commit outcome. Every code links there
    directly, so <code>https://stratadb.org/e/&lt;code&gt;</code> resolves for any of the{' '}
    {errors.length} of them, and the binary prints that URL in the error itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    {raised.size} of them are declared by a command in the catalog, so each{' '}
    <a href="/docs/reference">command page</a> lists the codes it can raise alongside their retry policy
    and commit outcome. The rest come from the runtime and the storage layer beneath the command surface.
  </p>
  <p>The same registry is available offline, matched to the version you installed:</p>
  <pre class="not-prose"><code>strata agents errors --json</code></pre>
</DocsLayout>
